# /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#
# Caddy gets and renews the TLS certificate by itself. The only requirement is
# that the name below already points at this machine — a certificate cannot be
# issued for a name that resolves somewhere else.
#
# That is why the cutover in DEPLOY.md tests on a SPARE name first: the spare
# points here from the start, so everything can be proven end to end while the
# real name is still being served by the old host. Adding the real name is then
# the last step, and Caddy issues its certificate on the first request.

{
	# Replace with a real address — it is only used for expiry warnings.
	email varunupadhyay1669@gmail.com
}

# ── Test name. Point this at the box first and verify against it. ──
aws.matheinstein.com {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4000
	encode zstd gzip
	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/mathslive.log
		format console
	}
}

# ── The real name. Uncomment ONLY at cutover, after the tests pass. ──
# class.matheinstein.com {
# 	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:4000
# 	encode zstd gzip
# 	log {
# 		output file /var/log/caddy/mathslive.log
# 		format console
# 	}
# }

# Notes for whoever edits this next:
#
# WebSockets need no configuration. Caddy's reverse_proxy passes an Upgrade
# request through as a matter of course, which is what Socket.IO needs.
#
# Do NOT put Cloudflare's orange-cloud proxy in front of this host. It has
# broken long-lived WebSocket connections for this app before; grey-cloud
# (DNS only) is the setting that works.
